&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/23/44410&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Were Half of Venezuela’s Oil Revenues Really Stolen? — Part II”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/23/44411</id><title>The Eradication of Extreme Poverty by 2030: a Key UN goal will be a Missed Target</title><updated>2026-10-23T04:49:01-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/23/44411" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/The-Eradication_630.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/The-Eradication_630.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/The-Eradication_630-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/The-Eradication_630.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
UNITED NATIONS, October 23 (IPS)  - The international day for the eradication of poverty, commemorated last week, was a grim reminder that the UN will not achieve its highly-ambitious target of helping eradicate extreme poverty—“in all its forms”– by 2030.
